    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unions significantly advanced the American middle class by negotiating the 8-hour workday, establishing overtime pay, and setting the 5-day work week, which fundamentally reshaped the modern work-life balance. They also championed the adoption of FICA and the Fair Labor Standards Act to create a reliable national social safety net that reduced the extreme financial vulnerability of the past. Furthermore, by demanding actual wage increases tied to rising living costs, unions directly fueled the transition of hundreds of thousands of American families from struggling poverty to secure, home-owning stability. These collective bargaining victories formed the economic foundation that allowed a vast demographic to transition from artisanal laborers to a self-sufficient middle class over the course of the 20th century.",
