      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While unions undeniably championed workers' welfare, their influence also sparked significant political and economic conflicts that sometimes hindered rapid business expansion or led to intense labor disputes that could tempora rily disrupt production. Furthermore, the aggressive adversarial tactics employed by certain unions occasionally resulted in the loss of low-wage jobs, as companies shifted to automation or offshore production to bypass union regulations and those who benefited from strict enforcement policies. Additionally, some critics argue that the \"one big union\" model blurred the lines between organized and unorganized labor in ways that could marginalize certain segments of the workforce in the long run. Despite these complexities, the narrative of the 20th-century middle class remains heavily centered on the initial successes of that collective power.",
